---
import Article from '../../layouts/Article.astro'
import AssertionCard from '../../components/AssertionCard.astro'
import ChartFigure from '../../components/ChartFigure.astro'
import { assertion } from '../../lib/feeds'
import { bar, line } from '../../lib/chart-spec'

const eightyfour = assertion('eighty-four-items-in-six-thousand-six-hundred')
const sources = assertion('thirty-of-thirty-two-sources-are-federal')
const vendor = assertion('the-nineteen-sixty-seven-volume-named-its-vendor')

const nixon = assertion('nixon-ran-twelve-points-ahead-of-his-state-here')
const different = assertion('the-state-and-the-county-chose-different-parties')
const noloser = assertion('the-volume-prints-no-loser')
const metro = assertion('the-county-was-read-from-the-metropolitan-table')

const reagan = assertion('reagan-took-seventy-three-per-cent-of-this-county')
const perot = assertion('perot-took-one-vote-in-six-and-the-republican-still-won-a-majority')
const threeseventy = assertion('one-of-three-hundred-and-seventy-counties')
const collapse = assertion('perots-collapse-did-not-go-to-the-democrat')
const turnout = assertion('the-turnout-did-not-move-for-sixteen-years')

const fourteen = assertion('the-same-fourteen-points-every-time')
const constant = assertion('the-county-takes-the-national-swing-and-adds-a-constant')
const mind = assertion('ohio-changed-its-mind-three-times-and-this-county-none')
const rank = assertion('the-rank-moves-and-the-gap-does-not')
const fortyseven = assertion('forty-seven-of-eighty-eight-counties-never-moved')
const putnam = assertion('putnam-is-above-it-every-time')

const agree2000 = assertion('three-compilations-agree-on-2000')
const witness = assertion('the-year-with-one-witness-now-has-two-that-disagree')
const tally = assertion('two-of-three-is-a-tally-and-not-a-canvass')

const twentyeight = assertion('twenty-eight-of-forty-two-elections')
const threetenths = assertion('nineteen-sixty-and-nineteen-eighty-are-three-tenths-apart')
const harding = assertion('harding-is-low-for-this-county')
const window = assertion('the-window-narrowed-from-nineteen-elections-to-eleven')

const allYears = Array.from({ length: 42 }, (_, i) => 1856 + i * 4)
const held: Record<number, number> = {
  1856: 46.9, 1860: 48.2, 1864: 54.3, 1868: 41.1, 1872: 44.6, 1876: 41.2,
  1880: 42.4, 1884: 42.7, 1888: 41.1, 1892: 38.0, 1896: 43.0, 1900: 43.9,
  1904: 52.5, 1908: 42.6, 1920: 53.6, 1960: 65.5, 1964: 51.2, 1980: 65.8,
  1984: 73.0, 1988: 69.0, 1992: 53.5, 1996: 55.3, 2000: 65.4, 2004: 66.1,
  2008: 59.6, 2012: 61.2, 2016: 66.5, 2020: 69.0,
}
const allShares = allYears.map((y) => (y in held ? held[y] : null))

const modernYears = [1980, 1984, 1988, 1992, 1996, 2000, 2004, 2008]
const allenShare = [65.8, 73.0, 69.0, 53.5, 55.3, 65.4, 66.1, 59.6]
const ohioShare = [51.5, 58.9, 55.0, 38.3, 41.0, 50.0, 50.8, 46.9]
const usShare = [50.7, 58.8, 53.4, 37.4, 40.7, 47.9, 50.7, 45.7]
const gapValues = [14.3, 14.1, 14.0, 15.2, 14.3, 15.4, 15.3, 12.7]

const putnamShare = [68.7, 78.3, 73.1, 54.9, 57.5, 74.0, 76.2, 70.0]
const hardinShare = [61.5, 69.1, 62.8, 44.3, 46.4, 59.0, 63.0, 59.1]

const seriesSpec = line(allYears, [{ name: 'Allen County', y: allShares }], {
  yTitle: 'Republican share of the vote cast (per cent)',
  xTitle: 'Presidential election',
})

const threeSpec = line(
  modernYears,
  [
    { name: 'Allen County', y: allenShare },
    { name: 'Ohio', y: ohioShare },
    { name: 'United States', y: usShare },
  ],
  { yTitle: 'Republican share of the vote cast (per cent)', labelLast: true },
)

const offsetSpec = bar(
  modernYears.map(String),
  gapValues,
  { yTitle: 'Allen County less Ohio (percentage points)', labelValues: true },
)

const neighbourSpec = line(
  modernYears,
  [
    { name: 'Putnam', y: putnamShare },
    { name: 'Allen', y: allenShare },
    { name: 'Hardin', y: hardinShare },
  ],
  { yTitle: 'Republican share of the vote cast (per cent)', labelLast: true },
)
---

<Article slug="the-same-fourteen-points">
  <p>
    The presidential returns for Allen County collected here run from 1856 to 1920 and then start
    again at 2000. In between, twenty-one elections, one of them read. The gap covers the New Deal,
    the war, the whole of the post-war realignment — everything, in short, that would explain how a
    county that voted Democratic in eleven of the twelve elections from 1856 to 1900 became one that
    now gives Republican presidential candidates two votes in three.
  </p>
  <p>
    Two of those elections and five more turn out to be sitting in federal reference books that
    nobody would look in for an election. Both books say, on their own pages, that the vote in them
    is not the government's.
  </p>

  <h2>The one table they bought</h2>
  <AssertionCard assertion={eightyfour} open />
  <p>
    The Census Bureau's last county compendium is 6,659 numbers about every county in the country —
    births, bank deposits, farm receipts, crime, wages, water use. One table in it carries a
    copyright notice, and that table is the vote.
  </p>
  <AssertionCard assertion={sources} />
  <AssertionCard assertion={vendor} />
  <p>
    There is a reason, and it is structural rather than lazy. Counties canvass the presidential
    vote; they certify it to a secretary of state; and no federal body collects what they certify.
    The federal publication of the presidential vote — the Clerk of the House's <em>Statistics of
    the Presidential and Congressional Elections</em> — is at the grain of the state and the
    congressional district. A federal volume that wants a county column has to buy one, and both of
    these did.
  </p>

  <h2>A county the state stopped agreeing with</h2>
  <AssertionCard assertion={nixon} open />
  <AssertionCard assertion={different} />
  <p>
    1964 is the sharper of the two. Johnson took Ohio by more than twenty-five points and lost
    Allen County. Whatever moved this county away from the Democrats, it had finished moving before
    the largest Democratic landslide of the century arrived, and the landslide did not reach here.
  </p>
  <AssertionCard assertion={noloser} />
  <AssertionCard assertion={metro} />
  <p>
    That second one is worth a sentence for its own sake. The volume's optical transcription slips
    the last three columns of every county row down by one, so the numbers printed under a county's
    name belong to the county above it. The reason this county's figures could be trusted anyway is
    that the same volume tabulates metropolitan areas in a separate table, and in 1960 the Lima
    metropolitan area was Allen County and nothing else — so the book prints these three numbers
    twice, in two layouts, and only one of them is broken.
  </p>

  <h2>Five more elections</h2>
  <AssertionCard assertion={reagan} open />
  <p>
    The record for the largest margin in this county was being credited to 2020. It belongs to 1984,
    by 2,363 votes, and the reason nobody here knew that is that 1984 was in the gap.
  </p>
  <AssertionCard assertion={perot} />
  <AssertionCard assertion={threeseventy} />
  <p>
    1992 is the most interesting year in the run. Nationally it was a three-cornered wreck: Bush
    took 37.4 per cent of the vote and lost. In Allen County he took an outright majority, one of
    fewer than four hundred counties in the country where he managed it.
  </p>
  <AssertionCard assertion={collapse} />
  <AssertionCard assertion={turnout} />

  <h2>The same fourteen points</h2>
  <p>
    Holding eight consecutive elections for the county, the state and the country in one file makes
    a comparison possible that no single return supports: not how Republican this county is, but how
    much more Republican than everybody else, election after election.
  </p>
  <AssertionCard assertion={fourteen} open />
  <ChartFigure
    id="three"
    title="Republican share of the presidential vote, 1980–2008"
    caption="The three lines move together. The distance between the top one and the middle one is the subject of this section."
    columns={['Year', 'Allen County', 'Ohio', 'United States']}
    rows={modernYears.map((y, i) => [
      String(y),
      `${allenShare[i]}%`,
      `${ohioShare[i]}%`,
      `${usShare[i]}%`,
    ])}
  />
  <script type="application/json" data-chart-spec="three" set:html={JSON.stringify(threeSpec)} is:inline />
  <ChartFigure
    id="offset"
    title="How far Allen County ran ahead of Ohio"
    caption="Eight elections, a range of 2.7 points. Over the same eight the county's own share moved 19.5."
    columns={['Year', 'Points ahead of Ohio']}
    rows={modernYears.map((y, i) => [String(y), gapValues[i].toFixed(1)])}
  />
  <script type="application/json" data-chart-spec="offset" set:html={JSON.stringify(offsetSpec)} is:inline />
  <AssertionCard assertion={constant} />
  <p>
    A county whose share of the vote swings twenty points while its distance from its own state
    barely moves is not deciding these elections on anything that happens in it. Whatever the
    argument is in a given year — inflation, a war, a third candidate — this county has it on the
    same terms as everybody else and then adds fourteen points.
  </p>
  <AssertionCard assertion={mind} />
  <AssertionCard assertion={fortyseven} />
  <AssertionCard assertion={rank} />
  <p>
    The rank is the least informative number in this section and the most tempting one. Third of
    eighty-eight in 1992 and twenty-sixth in 2008 describes how the other counties bunched, not how
    this one voted; the gap says the county did the same thing both times.
  </p>
  <AssertionCard assertion={putnam} />
  <ChartFigure
    id="neighbours"
    title="Allen County between two of its neighbours"
    caption="Putnam above at all eight, Hardin below at all eight, and Allen tracking both. The three lines are the same shape."
    columns={['Year', 'Putnam', 'Allen', 'Hardin']}
    rows={modernYears.map((y, i) => [
      String(y),
      `${putnamShare[i]}%`,
      `${allenShare[i]}%`,
      `${hardinShare[i]}%`,
    ])}
  />
  <script type="application/json" data-chart-spec="neighbours" set:html={JSON.stringify(neighbourSpec)} is:inline />

  <h2>What a third witness said</h2>
  <p>
    The compendium overlaps the modern series by three elections, which turns two compilations into
    three on those years. It came from a lineage with nothing in common with either of the others —
    a commercial publisher's series, rather than a university lab or a volunteer transcription
    project — so where it agrees, the agreement is worth something.
  </p>
  <AssertionCard assertion={agree2000} open />
  <AssertionCard assertion={witness} />
  <AssertionCard assertion={tally} />
  <p>
    Two of three is a tally. No county board's certificate for 2008 is held here, and none of the
    three files is the office that issued one, so the question of which figure is right stays open
    and gains a note explaining that it is open on principle rather than for want of evidence.
  </p>

  <h2>Where the turn still is</h2>
  <AssertionCard assertion={twentyeight} open />
  <ChartFigure
    id="series"
    title="Allen County's Republican share at every presidential election, 1856–2020"
    caption="Twenty-eight points and fourteen gaps. The 1864 figure is the county history's own estimate. The 1960 and 1964 points are the leading party's share, which for a county the Republican carried is the same quantity."
    columns={['Year', 'Republican share']}
    rows={allYears.map((y) => [String(y), y in held ? `${held[y]}%` : 'not held'])}
  />
  <script type="application/json" data-chart-spec="series" set:html={JSON.stringify(seriesSpec)} is:inline />
  <AssertionCard assertion={threetenths} />
  <AssertionCard assertion={harding} />
  <p>
    Harding was an Ohio newspaper publisher running against another Ohio newspaper publisher, and
    he carried this county by 2,320 votes. Read against everything now known to have come after it,
    that is a modest performance — lower than all but two of the thirteen elections that follow.
    Whether 1920 is the turn or a stop on the way to it is exactly what the missing years would
    settle.
  </p>
  <AssertionCard assertion={window} />
  <p>
    Eleven elections, 1912 to 1956. The Ohio Secretary of State's own returns are digitized to 1920
    and no further; the federal compendia that carry the vote begin at 1960; the two editions of the
    county data book that would hold 1968 and 1976 are not digitized at all. The answer to when this
    county turned Republican is in a printed volume in a library, and not on any wire reachable from
    here.
  </p>
</Article>
